<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-800-measurement`>>

!! The "~800" measurement

* Raw lines in the twin spans, both files, C1-C3, C6-C12 (without C4, C5, C13-C15): browser about 420, node about 410: about 830. The prior reader's "~800" matches this as lines TOUCHED across both files.
* Net lines REMOVED after the cuts land is smaller: glue code (non-comment) twin lines are about 255 browser + about 270 node = about 525. The shared stages add back about 180-200 code lines. Net deletion about 330 code lines, about 500 raw with the comments that go with them.
* Add C13 (founding/admit): about 76 browser + about 110 node code, net about -100 once the port stands. Total with C13: about -430 code lines.
* C14 moves about 80 code lines browser -> tw5 and nets roughly 0 (a lift, not a collapse).
So: "800 collapsed" overstates by about 2x; "800 touched" is right.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/per-candidate-answers`>>

!! Per-candidate answers (items 3-10, grouped; the batch is one function so most items are shared)

# State read/written: bootUnderLock closes over ~25 lets: relayPeers, fedGate, siblings, siblingGate, fleetShadows, fleetBoardFollowed, fleetRead, fleetMoves, oracleAtBoot, activeSurfaceId, vmManager, portFaceId, wikiActivation, daemon, wikiSense, slotActiveWikiId, crossroadsOfferingsHandle, bootKeys/bootKeyWrites/bootstrap, relayAdapter, replayManifest. Reads: IndexedDB (boot keys, custody rows, face rows), OPFS CAS, Web Lock, console. No wall clock decides anything (fleet read is a causal change-listener). Module globals: none beyond imports.
# Callers: openBrowserVessel only (-> guardedBoot -> bootUnderLock); openBrowserVessel is called by packages/lararium-web/src/main.ts and 9 browser tests. Callees are listed in the table.
# Tests: no unit test runs past catalog-ready/bootstrap-kept (wire-vessel-rig.ts BootHalted at "catalog-ready"; browser-device-edge.test.ts at "bootstrap-kept"). So island, climb, found/admit, dyads, catalog are reached through a halted boot; the daemon open, face proof, ring, sibling, verbs, pool, afterLive are reached only by tools/browser-weld/leaf-continuity.mjs and tests/live/two-tab-walk.mjs (real browser), plus source-grep "fault-pin" tests (nexus-board-climb-at-leaf.test.ts 310, 395). The tail has NO fast red today.
# House laws: no key on a surface (atom seeds zero-filled; vessel key broadcast as presence only, 1868); capability not host (C10 env read and C12 pools are the host-bound parts to keep in adapters); one owner per registry (registerInCatalog is declared twice, once per vessel: collapses into the C7 stage); no global now (fleet read, sibling lease are causal; the two timers stay in the pool); web3-only (n/a); old shapes burn (F1 cure must not add a back-compat read of a pre-snapshot bootstrap: a bootstrap lacking the island pair refuses by name or re-admits; ALPHA LINE).
# Risk: ORDER. Browser order is fixed by closures: island -> climb -> bootstrap(found/admit) -> pins -> crossing -> catalog -> mesh caps -> loadGenesis -> openDaemon (carry KEL -> face proof -> register planes -> daemonAuth -> plugin resolve -> fleet read -> openBrowserDaemonVm -> ring -> sibling). The ring and sibling need the daemon's `personaKel` and fleetShadows; moving the fleet read after the ring would read every key aside forever (fails closed, boot looks healthy, sibling lane dead). The sibling lane and the ring read `fedGate`/`siblingGate` through live closures (browserShareConfig reads at each decision); a stage that takes gates by value reopens the pre-crossing-null hazard that file's header documents. COLLISIONS: ralias (~/.lares-scratch/ralias/wt) edits open-node-vessel.ts (+72) and tw5 vessel-residency-wiring.ts (+12): C5, C11, C12 and any node-side C-cut must wait for or rebase on ralias. u1 touches keyhive content-key-seat, mesh content-key-holder, node session-sign-door: no overlap with these spans.
# Red-first shape (per cut): C1/F1: boot-admit-then-reboot rig asserting equal nexusPubkey and a non-throwing KEL read (red today if F1 holds), control: a never-admitted leaf and a founded leaf keep own-key across reboots. C3/C10/C11: a parity law that drives the shared stage with a browser fake and a node fake and demands equal log lines and equal outputs (CLI test-laws pattern: a SEED, not a lone literal). C6: a two-fleet case where `rolled`/owing and ABSENCE differ, control the one-fleet known-frontier case where both agree. C7: forged lease slot raises nothing (control: signed advance raises) under both vessels. C8: an ensureActive refusal must leave activeSurfaceId and place no open-wiki (red today on the browser). C13: stage-order law: archive before bootstrap on both.
# Verdicts:
* C2 CUT-READY (already shared; only a name). C3 CUT-READY. C10 CUT-READY (herms injected; env stays in node). C11 CUT-READY after ralias lands. C12 offering-lookup half CUT-READY (trivial); pool half DO-NOT-CUT.
* C8 CUT-READY (cures F2; browser surface hook injected).
* C6 NEEDS-RULING: fork = the browser's single-fleet `stands===true` vs node's edgeStandingAsOf/absence-is-not-clear. Lean: adopt the node reading in the shared stage (the stricter law), and say so in the browser witness.
* C7 NEEDS-RULING: fork = does the browser grow the relay-plane registration and CustodyLocked path (full-peer role) or does the stage take them as optional ports. Lean: optional ports, no browser behaviour change now.
* C1 NEEDS-RULING: fork = persist the island snapshot in the browser bootstrap (F1 cure) as part of the cut vs ship the cut first. Lean: red F1 first, cure inside the C1 stage; one PR.
* C13 NEEDS-RULING: fork = the FaceCustodyPort surface and the fixed write order. Lean: archive -> anchor -> bootstrap (the browser's order; the bootstrap names the admit, so it lands last). Largest port; sequence last.
* C14 NEEDS-RULING: fork = whether node gains persona/face verbs in the same PR (a capability add) or the lift lands browser-first with node later. Lean: lift first, add node after the stage stands.
* C4 DO-NOT-CUT (F3 is a policy fork, not a twin). C15 DO-NOT-CUT. C5 done.

Suggested stage order: F1 red -> C8 -> C3 -> C10 -> C2/C12-lookup -> C11 -> C6 -> C7 (+C1 with F1 cure) -> C13 -> C14. Each PR deletes its browser twin in the same PR (T13 rule).

